subsections.body_md:
[`Session::spawn`](code:src/session.rs#L466-L520) opens a PTY at [`UNSEEN_SIZE`](code:src/session.rs#L64), 120 columns by 40 rows, which stays its size until a viewer sizes it. The program starts with its environment cleared and set to the one [`env::for_session`](code:src/env.rs#L62) builds, which names the session in `CRYSTAL_SESSION` so its hooks can find it. The daemon drops its copy of the terminal's other end at once, so that the program's exit ends the output, and waits for the program by its pid, the same way a daemon it's handed over to does.

A thread runs [`Term::pump`](code:src/session.rs#L2747-L2766) for each session, reading 16 KiB at a time through [`handover::readers`](code:src/handover.rs#L326), which a handover can stop mid-read. Each read goes through [`Screen::take`](code:src/session.rs#L2793) under the screen's lock:

- the daemon's own [`vt::Screen`](code:src/vt.rs#L113), an `alacritty_terminal` grid with its history, takes it first; it's what `crystal read`, the rules that read agents' screens and a new viewer catching up all see;
- the [`OutputRing`](code:src/output_ring.rs#L25) keeps the last [`MOST`](code:src/output_ring.rs#L15) bytes with a mark each second, for `crystal read --since` ([`OutputRing::since`](code:src/output_ring.rs#L108));
- every [`Viewer`](code:src/viewer.rs#L13) gets the same bytes, so its own screen stays in step: `crystal attach` and the TUI's panes are both viewers.

A program asking its terminal something (where the cursor is, what colours it has) is answered from the daemon's screen, and the answer written back to the PTY, so a program asks no viewer and never gets two answers. The screen also counts bells and keeps the last text a program asked to copy (OSC 52), for a viewer to pass on.

`crystal restart-server` asks the running daemon to become the newly installed crystal: [`restart_daemon`](code:src/client.rs#L625) sends `Request::Handover` with the new binary and the [`FORMAT`](code:src/handover.rs#L52) it reads, from [`hand_over`](code:src/client.rs#L654). The daemon's side is [`Daemon::hand_over`](code:src/daemon.rs#L1054-L1092).

What can be refused is refused before anything changes: [`handover::check`](code:src/handover.rs#L140) runs the new binary to ask which format it reads, and a different one sends the client back to restart the daemon cold, the way it always did. Otherwise the gate closes ([`Gate::close`](code:src/handover.rs#L391)): connections that come in now wait for the next crystal, and requests already being answered get up to [`HANDOVER_GRACE`](code:src/daemon.rs#L121) to finish.

[`exec_handed_over`](code:src/daemon.rs#L1096-L1168) then gets everything ready:

1. the sessions and flow runs are written to the database first, with [`save_sessions_and_runs`](code:src/db.rs#L477), so whatever goes wrong from here, the next daemon starts them again;
2. helpers like the distiller are waited for, and every output reader is stopped;
3. each session gives up what carries it on with [`Session::hand_over`](code:src/session.rs#L2116): its PTY's descriptor, its screen and history, its agent's state;
4. all of it goes into a [`State`](code:src/handover.rs#L57), written by [`handover::write`](code:src/handover.rs#L154) to a file that's unlinked as soon as it's made, so the sessions' environments are never left on disk;
5. the descriptors to keep are marked with [`keep_across_exec`](code:src/handover.rs#L183), and [`handover::exec`](code:src/handover.rs#L220) runs the new crystal in this process.

The pid stays the same, so every program the daemon started is still its child and waiting for one still says how it ended; the listening socket never closes, so a client only waits. The new crystal's [`daemon::run`](code:src/daemon.rs#L130) reads the state and calls [`take_over`](code:src/daemon.rs#L1174), which brings each session back with [`Session::adopt`](code:src/session.rs#L2179) and answers the clients that asked. herdr hands its sockets to a new process instead; that keeps the old daemon alive until the new one has taken over, but its programs stop being the daemon's children, which is why crystal execs.

